WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:17.306
This video shows how several production plans can be evaluated at once using matrix algebra. In the last video we used a matrix-vector product to work out the total raw material requirement for one production plan. Now we look at several production plans at the same time.

2
00:00:19.256 --> 00:00:22.112
The learning goals of this video are the following.

3
00:00:23.812 --> 00:00:29.881
First: to gather several production vectors, column by column, into one matrix.

4
00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:38.270
Second: to understand a matrix-matrix product as several matrix-vector products at once.

5
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.120
And third: to check the dimensions and the meaning of a matrix-matrix product.

6
00:00:47.170 --> 00:01:01.042
We stay with the ice cream workshop. The matrix A still holds the raw material requirement per batch: the raw materials in the rows, the products in the columns. This arrangement stays the same throughout the example.

7
00:01:01.742 --> 00:01:11.465
In the last video we looked at a vector x that says what quantity of each kind of ice cream is to be produced, in other words the production plan.

8
00:01:11.865 --> 00:01:19.939
We now call this vector x^(1). The one in the raised index is not a power; it is used for numbering.

9
00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:24.217
The production plan holds the same quantities as in the last video.

10
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:30.863
The product A · x^(1) gives the raw material requirement for carrying out the production plan.

11
00:01:31.463 --> 00:01:38.330
The result was seventy litres of milk, forty-six kilograms of fruit and thirty-eight bars of chocolate.

12
00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:48.235
Now suppose we want to look not at one production plan but at three different ones, for three consecutive weeks, say.

13
00:01:48.835 --> 00:02:05.192
Each of these vectors describes one complete production plan. In all three vectors, its three entries stand in the same order for soft serve, sundaes and sorbet. So the position of an entry carries the same meaning in all three vectors.

14
00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:15.574
The raised index in brackets numbers whole production plans, not individual entries. A single entry would get a lowered index, as in the second video.

15
00:02:17.424 --> 00:02:22.903
How can we work out the raw material requirement for all three production plans together?

16
00:02:23.853 --> 00:02:28.973
We could compute a matrix-vector product separately for each production plan.

17
00:02:29.973 --> 00:02:36.528
That would be correct, but it would write the same calculation step down three separate times.

18
00:02:37.028 --> 00:02:43.499
And notice: the requirements matrix A stays the same every time.

19
00:02:44.399 --> 00:02:46.660
Only the production vector changes.

20
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.859
It is exactly this repetition that we now want to bundle into a single calculation.

21
00:02:54.659 --> 00:02:59.502
We can gather the three production vectors column by column into one matrix.

22
00:03:00.602 --> 00:03:02.810
We call this matrix B.

23
00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.497
Each of the earlier vectors is one column of B.

24
00:03:07.547 --> 00:03:12.848
As in the production vectors before, the rows of B stand for the three kinds of ice cream.

25
00:03:13.948 --> 00:03:25.754
So every entry of B depends on two characteristics: on the product and on the production plan. That is exactly why B is a matrix and not a vector.

26
00:03:28.604 --> 00:03:44.590
Instead of computing three separate matrix-vector products, we can multiply the requirements matrix A directly by the matrix B of production plans. So the right-hand matrix bundles exactly the three vectors we would otherwise insert one after another.

27
00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:56.763
We can understand the matrix-matrix product here as several matrix-vector products at once. Every column of B is treated just like the single production vector from the last video.

28
00:03:57.263 --> 00:04:02.131
The vertical bars in the formula: they are simply there to separate the columns.

29
00:04:03.131 --> 00:04:06.878
A · B is a matrix with three columns here.

30
00:04:07.478 --> 00:04:11.933
To work out the result, A is first multiplied by the first column of B.

31
00:04:12.533 --> 00:04:17.248
We write the result of that as the first column of the result matrix.

32
00:04:17.848 --> 00:04:21.610
Next, A is multiplied by the second column of B.

33
00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:26.127
We write this result as the second column of the result matrix.

34
00:04:26.727 --> 00:04:32.734
Finally, A is multiplied by the third column of B, and that result is written as the third column.

35
00:04:35.584 --> 00:04:40.216
Let us now work out the first entry of the result matrix in detail.

36
00:04:40.816 --> 00:04:52.864
For that we look at the first row of A and the first column of B. We set the column of B upright above the row of A: the topmost entry above the left column, the bottom one above the right.

37
00:04:53.364 --> 00:05:03.074
So the scheme is exactly the one from the matrix-vector product. Four times ten, plus five times six, plus zero times four gives seventy.

38
00:05:03.724 --> 00:05:08.393
That is seventy litres of milk for the first production plan.

39
00:05:10.293 --> 00:05:17.454
To work out entry two, one of the result matrix, we look at the second row of A and still at the first column of B.

40
00:05:18.154 --> 00:05:24.884
One times ten, plus two times six, plus six times four gives forty-six.

41
00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:30.152
That is forty-six kilograms of fruit for the first production plan.

42
00:05:32.052 --> 00:05:37.341
As a third example, let us work out entry one, two of the result matrix.

43
00:05:38.291 --> 00:05:41.706
For that we again take the first row of A.

44
00:05:42.556 --> 00:05:44.836
But now the second column of B.

45
00:05:46.536 --> 00:05:52.008
Four times eight, plus five times ten, plus zero times five gives eighty-two.

46
00:05:52.658 --> 00:05:57.055
That is eighty-two litres of milk for the second production plan.

47
00:05:57.805 --> 00:06:09.010
This principle holds for every entry of the result matrix: the row of the left-hand matrix determines the raw material, and the column of the right-hand matrix determines the production plan.

48
00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:15.337
The remaining entries come about in the same pattern.

49
00:06:16.787 --> 00:06:22.104
As the last step of this calculation, we should label the result matrix.

50
00:06:22.604 --> 00:06:30.170
The rows of the result always carry the same meaning as the rows of the left-hand matrix, so here the raw materials.

51
00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:38.799
The columns of the result always carry the same meaning as the columns of the right-hand matrix, so here the production plans.

52
00:06:41.499 --> 00:06:55.382
A matrix-matrix product comes with conditions, just as the matrix-vector product did: first, whether it can be computed at all in terms of format, and second, whether it can make sense in terms of content.

53
00:06:56.132 --> 00:07:02.411
For the format we look at the dimensions here. A has three rows and three columns.

54
00:07:03.261 --> 00:07:06.832
B also has three rows and three columns.

55
00:07:07.682 --> 00:07:13.369
The two inner numbers agree, so the format here is suitable for the multiplication.

56
00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:17.266
The outer numbers determine the dimension of the result.

57
00:07:17.766 --> 00:07:22.103
The result matrix has three rows and three columns.

58
00:07:24.903 --> 00:07:39.306
In general, an m × n matrix can be multiplied by an n × r matrix. The result is then an m × r matrix. Here m and r may differ; only the two inner dimension numbers n have to agree.

59
00:07:41.506 --> 00:07:53.897
That settles the question of format. The second condition concerns the content: the meanings have to fit together as well. This check is the content-side counterpart of the matching inner dimensions.

60
00:07:54.597 --> 00:08:05.104
The columns of A stand for the products, and the rows of B stand for the products as well. It is exactly this shared meaning that links the two inner dimensions.

61
00:08:05.804 --> 00:08:12.820
The rows of the left-hand matrix A become the rows of the result, so here the raw materials.

62
00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:26.071
And the columns of the right-hand matrix B become the columns of the result, here the production plans. So both the dimensions and the meanings of the result can be read off directly from the two factors.

63
00:08:29.871 --> 00:08:40.561
In a matrix-matrix product we look at the right-hand matrix column by column.

64
00:08:41.561 --> 00:08:46.621
With every column of the right-hand matrix, one matrix-vector product is formed.

65
00:08:47.621 --> 00:08:52.741
The result matrix here shows the raw material requirements of the different production plans.

66
00:08:53.341 --> 00:08:59.448
So the matrix-matrix product extends the idea of the matrix-vector product: instead of evaluating just one production plan, we can process several production plans at the same time.
